.TH DNSBENCH 1 "2026-09-02" "dnsbench 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dnsbench \- rank DNS resolvers from your own connection, including CDN edge quality
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dnsbench
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B dnsbench update
.br
.B dnsbench history
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dnsbench
measures and ranks DNS resolvers from the link it is run on, over UDP, TCP,
DNS over TLS and DNS over HTTPS, and reports a composite score with published
weights.
.PP
Besides lookup latency it measures
.BR "CDN edge quality" :
it resolves a set of DNS\-steered CDN hostnames through every resolver and
times a TCP connection to whichever address came back, so a resolver that
answers quickly and sends you to an edge on another continent is visible as
such. That column is
.B EDGE
and it is the reason the tool exists.
.PP
Measured facts and facts a provider declares about itself are never mixed. A
badge written
.B +DNSSEC
or
.B \-ads
was established by a probe during this run; a badge written
.BI ~ nolog
is a claim by the operator, is styled differently, and contributes to no
measured subscore.
.PP
A figure that was not measured is printed as a dash and emitted as
.BR null ,
never as zero: zero is the best value in every latency column.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " name"
Weight profile for the composite score: \fBbalanced\fR, \fBspeed\fR,
\fBprivacy\fR, \fBstreaming\fR or \fBgaming\fR. Default \fBbalanced\fR. The
weights are printed in the header of every output format and travel in the
JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " keys"
Comma\-separated catalog keys, or addresses of the machine's own resolvers.
Only these are measured. Bypasses
.BR \-\-near ,
since it is already an explicit, short list.
.TP
.BI \-\-catalog " name"
.B embedded
or
.BR dnscrypt .
Default
.BR embedded .
.B dnscrypt
opts into the cached DNSCrypt public\-resolvers list; see
.B UPDATE
below. A provider key already curated in the embedded catalog is never
replaced by the DNSCrypt list's copy of the same key.
.TP
.BI \-\-require " tags"
Comma\-separated tags every measured provider must carry, from the tag
vocabulary in
.IR docs/DATA.md .
Applied after the catalogs are merged.
.TP
.B \-\-near
Reachability pre\-pass for
.BR "\-\-catalog dnscrypt" :
a TCP connect against every candidate that has an address to dial, paced the
same way the measurement plan is, keeping the fastest 25 for the full
battery instead of every listed resolver. A candidate with nothing to dial
is kept unconditionally.
.TP
.BI \-\-rounds " n"
Measured rounds per provider. Default 5. A round is one pass over the domain
set, and an additional round 0 is sent and discarded because it pays for cache
fill and route setup that no later query pays.
.TP
.BI \-\-probes " names"
Comma\-separated, from \fBwarm\fR, \fBtcp\fR, \fBcold\fR, \fBecs\fR,
\fBdot-fresh\fR, \fBdot-warm\fR, \fBdoh\fR, \fBdnssec\fR and \fBfilter\fR.
Default \fBwarm\fR. See
.B PROBES
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " name"
\fBtable\fR, \fBjson\fR, \fBcsv\fR or \fBmarkdown\fR. Default \fBtable\fR.
Only \fBjson\fR carries a stability guarantee; do not parse the others.
.TP
.BI \-\-history " path"
Append the run to a JSONL history file. Every line carries the network
fingerprint, so runs on different links are never silently mixed.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " ms"
Per\-query timeout in milliseconds. Default 2000 for plaintext probes;
encrypted probes use 5000, because a handshake is two more round trips.
.TP
.BI \-\-cold\-zone " zone"
Wildcard zone the \fBcold\fR probe asks under. Without it the probe is skipped
and the run says so, rather than generating NXDOMAIN traffic against a third
party.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-bundle " path"
CA bundle for DoT and DoH, overriding the system cascade. The trust anchor is
always the system store; nothing is embedded.
.TP
.B \-\-tui
Watch the run in a full\-screen terminal interface. Falls back to the plain
table, saying why, when \fBTERM\fR is unset or \fBdumb\fR or the output is not
a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-palette " name"
\fBdefault\fR or \fBcolorblind\fR, which swaps green and red for blue and
orange. TUI only.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Plain text in the TUI, as the \fBNO_COLOR\fR environment variable does.
.TP
.BI \-\-region " code"
\fBglobal\fR, \fBsa\fR, \fBna\fR, \fBeu\fR, \fBapac\fR, \fBaf\fR or \fBme\fR.
Default is detected.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-geo
Do not look up the public address, the ASN or the region. The run reports
\fBregion: global\fR and a null ASN, and sends no packet other than the
measurement.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Measure even with a tunnel interface up. Without it the run refuses, because
every number would describe the tunnel rather than the link.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " n"
Fix the shuffle, for a reproducible plan.
.TP
.BI \-\-watch " duration"
Repeat the run at a fixed interval instead of running once, printing and, with
.BR \-\-history ,
appending each measurement. A duration is a positive integer followed by
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
.BR h ,
.B d
or
.BR w ,
for example
.IR 15m .
A tick that fails does not stop the loop. Alerts when the winning provider
changes, and, with
.BR \-\-alert\-edge ,
when a provider's edge penalty crosses that threshold; both go to standard
error. Refused together with
.BR \-\-tui .
.TP
.BI \-\-watch\-count " n"
Stop
.B \-\-watch
after
.I n
measurements. Without it the loop runs until interrupted.
.TP
.BI \-\-alert\-edge " ms"
With
.BR \-\-watch ,
alert when any provider's median edge penalty exceeds this many milliseconds.
Off by default.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and the commit it was built from.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the flag list the binary actually accepts.
.SH UPDATE
.PP
.B dnsbench update
fetches the DNSCrypt public\-resolvers list and verifies its minisign signature
against a key that ships in the binary, then caches both under
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/dnsbench/ .
.PP
Verification is mandatory and there is no flag to skip it. A file that fails it
is discarded, whatever was cached before is left alone, and the exit status is
4. The transport is not trusted and does not need to be: the signature is what
establishes integrity, and a mirror that lies is caught.
.PP
The list is
.B not
used by default and never becomes the default. Its own header warns that it
includes servers which censor, which do not validate DNSSEC, and which collect
and monetise queries, so ranking them all by latency and crowning a winner would
be irresponsible. Opt in explicitly with
.BR "\-\-catalog dnscrypt" ,
optionally narrowed with
.B \-\-require
or pre\-filtered for reachability with
.BR \-\-near .
Only DoH stamps in the list become providers; a DNSCrypt\-protocol entry names
an address no transport in this tool speaks.
.SH HISTORY
.PP
.B dnsbench history
.RI [ options ]
reads a JSONL history file back and either prints an aggregated table or, with
.BR \-\-plot ,
a sparkline.
.TP
.BI \-\-last " duration"
Only runs within this window before now: a positive integer followed by
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
.BR h ,
.B d
or
.BR w .
.TP
.BI \-\-asn " asn"
Only this network, for example
.IR AS27699 .
.TP
.BI \-\-provider " key"
Only this provider.
.TP
.B \-\-plot
Sparkline of p50 over time instead of the aggregated table. Requires
.BR \-\-provider :
a sparkline is one series, and a provider can appear on more than one
network.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " path"
History file to read. Default
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/dnsbench/runs.jsonl .
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-plot ,
the default view is one row per network and provider: how many runs, the
mean/min/max p50, and the latest score. A network, cold mode, domain set or
probe is never averaged in with another; the network fingerprint on every
line is why a fibre run and a mobile run on the same machine are never mixed
into one meaningless number.
.SH PROBES
.TP
.B warm
Repeated lookups of a fixed domain set. The everyday case, and the probe the
ranking is built on.
.TP
.B tcp
The same question over TCP, which is the fallback path a truncated answer
takes.
.TP
.B cold
A name nobody has ever asked for, drawn fresh per query under
.BR \-\-cold\-zone ,
so every query is a cache miss by construction and the resolver has to recurse.
.TP
.B ecs
Resolves a set of DNS\-steered CDN hostnames through each resolver and times a
TCP connection to the answer. Reports the median penalty against the best edge
any resolver reached this run, and how many hosts came back far enough adrift
to have been answered from the wrong region.
.TP
.B dot-fresh
DNS over TLS, timing connect, handshake and query together. A benchmark that
opens a connection per query is measuring handshakes; this variant exists to
show by how much.
.TP
.B dot-warm
DNS over TLS on a connection opened once and held, which is what a real stub
resolver does. Only this variant feeds the score.
.TP
.B doh
DNS over HTTPS, RFC 8484, over HTTP/1.1. Every result records
\fBhttp_version\fR, because an HTTP/1.1 measurement is not comparable to a
browser's real HTTP/2 behaviour. An endpoint that serves HTTP/2 only is
recorded as refusing, never as unreachable.
.TP
.B dnssec
Asks a deliberately broken zone twice, once ordinarily and once with the
Checking Disabled bit, and reads validation out of the pair. A resolver that
answers neither way is unknown rather than failing.
.TP
.B filter
Asks for an advertising domain and reads whether the answer is a resolution or
a block.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Run completed.
.TP
.B 1
Run completed with errors, or was interrupted; partial results were emitted.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
No provider reachable, most likely no connectivity.
.TP
.B 4
Catalog verification failure.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set to any value to drop every colour escape from the terminal interface.
.TP
.B TERM
Unset or \fBdumb\fR sends \fB\-\-tui\fR to the plain table.
.TP
.B TZ
Read only as the last step of region detection, when the lookup was skipped or
did not answer.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where
.B dnsbench update
writes. Defaults to
.IR ~/.cache .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the user provider override file is read from. Defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Where
.B dnsbench history
reads from by default, and the conventional
.B \-\-history
target. Defaults to
.IR ~/.local/share .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/dnsbench/runs.jsonl
Conventional location for the history file. Nothing is written there unless
.B \-\-history
names it; read by
.B dnsbench history
unless
.B \-\-file
says otherwise.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/dnsbench/public-resolvers.md
The verified DNSCrypt catalog and its signature, written by
.B "dnsbench update"
and read by
.BR "\-\-catalog dnscrypt" .
Falls back to
.I ~/.cache
when the variable is unset.
.TP
.I ~/.config/dnsbench/providers.toml
User provider overrides, same schema as the embedded catalog, merged in by
key ahead of both the embedded and the DNSCrypt catalogs.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Rank every provider in the catalog over UDP:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dnsbench
.EE
.RE
.PP
The full battery, including the edge probe, watched as it happens:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dnsbench \-\-tui \-\-probes warm,cold,ecs,dot\-warm,dnssec,filter \\
         \-\-cold\-zone probe.dnsbench.esli.blog
.EE
.RE
.PP
Machine\-readable, into a history file, with no lookup of the network:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dnsbench \-\-no\-geo \-\-format json \\
         \-\-history ~/.local/share/dnsbench/runs.jsonl
.EE
.RE
.PP
Opt into the DNSCrypt catalog, pre\-filtered for reachability:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dnsbench update
dnsbench \-\-catalog dnscrypt \-\-near
.EE
.RE
.PP
Watch a resolver every 15 minutes and alert on a bad edge penalty:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dnsbench \-\-watch 15m \-\-alert\-edge 50 \-\-only quad9 \-\-probes warm,ecs \\
         \-\-history ~/.local/share/dnsbench/runs.jsonl
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read that history back as a sparkline:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dnsbench history \-\-provider quad9 \-\-plot
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B dnsbench
does not change your DNS settings, does not phone home, and carries no
telemetry. Besides the measurement it makes four DNS queries at startup to
name the network it is on and to check for transparent DNS interception,
which
.B \-\-no\-geo
removes. The public address those queries establish is used to look up the ASN
and is then discarded: the output carries the ASN and the operator's name and
never the address, so a result is safe to paste into a bug report. A mismatch
between the two independent address queries sets
.B dns_interception
in the output and is reported prominently, without stopping the run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kdig (1),
.BR dig (1),
.BR resolvectl (1)
.PP
The specification lives with the source, under
.IR docs/ :
methodology, scoring, the output contract and the data sets.
